e` (Needs mapping if app uses codes like \"PWC\")\n        *   Prop (Outboard) -\u003e `Propulsion Type`\n        *   Hull mat (Inflatable) -\u003e `Hull Material`\n        *   Craft Use (Personal) -\u003e `Craft Use` (Map \"Personal\" to \"Pleasure\"?)\n        *   Fuel (Petrol) -\u003e `Fuel Type` (Map \"Petrol\" to \"Gasoline\"?)\n    *   **Footer/Additional Info:** Emergency contacts (NCD Small Craft, NMSA Emergency, Water Police, St Johns), Logos (NCD Commission, NMSA, Motu Koita Assembly), Sponsor Logo (Ela Motors). *Self-correction: Are these static or dynamic? Likely static standard info for all certificates.*\n\n3.  **Define App Functionality:**\n    *   **Trigger:** When should the certificate be generated? Likely upon final approval of a registration by an authorized user (Admin/Registrar). Could also be an option to re-generate/download later from the approved craft\u0027s profile.\n    *   **Data Source:** The app needs to pull all the required data points identified in step 2 from the specific craft\u0027s registration record in the database.\n    *   **Template:** A predefined template (HTML, DOCX, or using a PDF generation library template) is needed to structure the certificate layout, including logos and static text.\n    *   **Generation:** A backend process will take the data and populate the template.\n    *   **Output Format:** PDF is the most standard and suitable format for official documents (prevents easy modification, consistent viewing).\n    *   **Saving:**\n        *   Option 1: Generate the PDF on demand each time it\u0027s requested. Store *metadata* about generation (who generated it, when).\n        *   Option 2: Generate the PDF once upon approval, save the actual PDF file linked to the registration record (e.g., in cloud storage like S3 or blob storage), and store the file path/reference in the database. *This seems better for auditability and consistency.*\n    *   **Export/Download:** Provide a button/link for authorized users (Admin, Registrar, maybe even the owner via a separate portal?) to download the generated PDF.\n\n4.  **Refine Data Mapping (App Fields -\u003e Certificate Fields):**\n    *   `SCA REGO No.` -\u003e Registration Number\n    *   `Registration Effective Date` -\u003e Registration Date\n    *   `Registration Expiry Date` -\u003e Registration Expiry Date\n    *   `Payment Receipt Number` -\u003e Receipt Number\n    *   *(Primary) Owner Name` -\u003e Owner Name on Certificate\n    *   *(Primary) Owner Address Fields` -\u003e Owner Address on Certificate (Need to format correctly)\n    *   `Make of Boat` -\u003e Make of boat\n    *   `Model` -\u003e Model\n    *   `Year` -\u003e Year\n    *   `Hull ID No. / Serial No.` -\u003e Hull ID No. Serial No:\n    *   `Color` -\u003e Colour\n    *   `Length` + Units -\u003e Length\n    *   `Vessel Type` (Map text if needed) -\u003e Type\n    *   `Propulsion Type` (Map text if needed) -\u003e Prop\n    *   `Hull Material` (Map text if needed) -\u003e Hull mat\n    *   `Craft Use` (Map text if needed) -\u003e Craft Use\n    *   `Fuel Type` (Map text if needed) -\u003e Fuel\n\n5.  **Address Multi-Owner Scenario:**\n    *   Decision: For simplicity on the certificate, list the designated \"Primary Owner\" name and address.\n    *   Alternative (more complex): Have a smaller section listing \"Co-Owners: [Name 1], [Name 2]\". This might clutter the certificate. Sticking with the Primary Owner seems pragmatic based on the single-owner example. The full ownership details are always available *within* the app record itself.\n\n6.  **Technical Implementation Considerations:**\n    *   **PDF Library:** Choose a suitable library for the backend language (e.g., `FPDF`/`TCPDF` for PHP, `ReportLab` for Python, `iTextSharp`/`QuestPDF` for .NET, `pdfkit`/`jsPDF` for Node.js).\n    *   **Templating Engine:** Using HTML+CSS rendered to PDF (via libraries like `wkhtmltopdf` or Puppeteer) offers good design flexibility.\n    *   **Storage:** Decide wher