      "text": "Okay, let\u0027s integrate the ability to generate, export, and save a Registration Certificate based on the provided example.\n\n**Functionality Required:**\n\n1.  **Certificate Generation:** The app needs a function to create a certificate document populated with data from an approved registration record.\n2.  **Template:** A predefined layout mirroring the example certificate is needed.\n3.  **Data Population:** Pull specific fields from the database record for the selected craft registration.\n4.  **Export:** Allow authorized users to download the certificate as a file (PDF is highly recommended).\n5.  **Database Storage:** Store a reference to the generated certificate (or the certificate itself, though referencing is often better) linked to the specific registration record.\n\n**Data Fields to Include on the Certificate (based on the example and our previous form analysis):**\n\n*   **Header/Branding:**\n    *   NCD Small Craft Registration Board Logo (Static Image)\n    *   \"National Capital District Commission\" (Static Text)\n    *   \"Motu Koita Assembly\" (Static Text)\n    *   \"Registration Certificate\" (Static Title)\n*   **Legislation:**\n    *   \"Small Craft Act 2011\" (Static Text)\n    *   \"Amended Schedules SI 190/2016\" (Static Text - *Confirm if this is always the same or needs to be configurable*)\n*   **Owner Details:**\n    *   Owner Name: From the `Owner Information` section (For multi-owner, display the **Primary Owner** here).\n    *   Owner Address: From the `Owner Information` section (Formatted Postal Address, Town/District, LLG/Ward/Village, Province - combine relevant fields).\n*   **Registration Details Block:**\n    *   Registration Number: From the `SCA REGO No.` field (assigned during approval).\n    *   Registration Date: From the `Registration Effective Date` field.\n    *   Registration Expiry Date: From the `Registration Expiry Date` field.\n    *   Receipt Number: From the `Payment Receipt Number` field.\n*   **Small Craft Details Block:**\n    *   Make of boat: From `Make of Boat` field.\n    *   Model: From `Model` field.\n    *   Year: From `Year` field.\n    *   Hull ID No. Serial No: From `Hull ID No. / Serial No.` field.\n    *   Colour: From `Color` field.\n    *   Length: From `Length` field (including units, e.g., \"3.2m\").\n    *   Type: From `Vessel Type` field (e.g., \"Rigid Inflatable\").\n    *   Prop: From `Propulsion Type` field (e.g., \"Outboard\").\n    *   Hull mat: From `Hull Material` field (e.g., \"Inflatable\").\n    *   Craft Use: From `Craft Use` field (e.g., \"Personal\" or \"Pleasure\").\n    *   Fuel: From `Fuel Type` field (e.g., \"Petrol\" or \"Gasoline\").\n*   **Footer/Static Info:**\n    *   Emergency Contact Numbers (NCD Small Craft, NMSA Emergency, Water Police, St Johns) - These are likely static and part of the template.\n    *   Logos (NCD Commission, NMSA, Motu Koita Assembly, Ela Motors) - Static images within the template. (*Confirm if sponsor logos like Ela Motors change or are fixed*).\n\n**Implementation Steps:**\n\n1.  **Create a Certificate Template:**\n    *   Design a template (e.g., using HTML/CSS, or a dedicated reporting tool template) that matches the layout of the example certificate.\n    *   Include placeholders for all the dynamic data fields listed above.\n    *   Embed the static text, logos, and footer information directly into the template.\n\n2.  **Develop the Generation Logic:**\n    *   Create a backend function/service that takes a `Registration ID` as input.\n    *   This function retrieves the approved registration record (including owner and craft details) from the database.\n    *   It maps the retrieved data fields to the corresponding placeholders in the certificate template.\n    *   It uses a PDF generation library (e.g., wkhtmltopdf, Puppeteer, ReportLab, iTextSharp, QuestPDF depending on your tech stack) to render the populated template into a PDF document.\n\n3.  **Implement Export Functionality:**\n    *   Add a \"Download Certificate\" button or link within the app\u0027s interface for approved registrations (visible to authorized roles like Admin, Registrar).\n    *   When clicked, this button triggers the generation logic (or retrieves a previously saved certificate).\n    *   The backend should return the generated PDF file with appropriate headers (e.g., `Content-Disposition: attachment; filename\u003d\"Registration_Certificate_[RegNo].pdf\"`) so the browser prompts the user to save it.\n\n4.  **Implement Database Saving:**\n    *   **Option A (Recommended): Store a Reference:**\n        *   After generating the PDF upon approval (or the first time it\u0027s requested), save the file to a secure location (e.g., cloud storage like AWS S3, Azure Blob Storage, or a dedicated file server directory).\n        *   Add a field to your `Registrations` table (or a related `Certificates` table) in the database, e.g., `certificate_file_path` or `certificate_storage_key`. Store the path/key/URL to the saved PDF file here.\n        *   Optionally, store metadata like `certificate_generated_at` and `generated_by_user_id`.\n        *   The \"Download Certificate\" button would then retrieve the file from storage using this reference.\n    *   **Option B: Store the File in DB (Less Ideal for large files):**\n        *   Add a `BLOB` (Binary Large Object) field to your database table to store the PDF file content directly.\n        *   This can make database backups larger and might impact performance. Generally avoided unless files are very small or there\u0027s a specific reason.\n\n5.  **User Interface:**\n    *   Ensure the relevant data fields used in the certificate are captured accurately during the registration process (as defined from the first form).\n    *   Provide the interface element (button/link) for authorized users to initiate the download.\n\n**Handling Multi-Owner:**\n\n*   As decided previously, the certificate template will likely display only the **Primary Owner\u0027s** name and address for clarity and space. The full ownership details remain accessible within the app\u0027s main registration record view.\n\nThis approach ensures that you can reliably generate consistent, professional-looking registration certificates directly from your application data, provide an easy way for users to export them, and maintain a record of the official certificate associated with each registration.",
